<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 11 and earlier backup rules.

  Three paths are excluded, for three different reasons.

  credentials/ holds the GitHub credential, encrypted under a key that lives in
  the Android Keystore and never leaves the device, so a restored copy on
  another device could not be decrypted anyway. Excluding it keeps that from
  being the user's problem to discover: they would see "Connected" against a
  credential that fails closed on first use. It also keeps the ciphertext out of
  Google's backup transport entirely, which is the stronger property.

  pocketclaw/ and picoclaw/ are the same thing at two points in time: Core's
  private state directory, holding config.json and .security.yml. Android
  onboarding declines credential encryption, so .security.yml holds provider API
  keys and channel bot tokens as plaintext. Those are 0600 and app-private,
  which stops another app reading them — but says nothing about backup, and
  without these exclusions they were copied verbatim into Google's cloud backup
  and into device-to-device transfer.

  pocketclaw/ is the canonical path. Its exclusion is here BEFORE any code
  creates that directory, which is the only ordering that is safe: a migration
  that ran first would leave secrets in an unprotected path for however long it
  took the rules to catch up, and an interrupted one would leave them there
  indefinitely.

  picoclaw/ is a LEGACY SECURITY EXCLUSION, deliberately retained. It is not an
  active product path any more, and it is not a Zero-Pico failure. A migration
  that is interrupted, or a downgrade to an older build, can leave sensitive
  files in the historical directory; Android must not be allowed to back those
  up merely because PocketClaw changed where it writes. It is removed only when
  the migration compatibility window is intentionally retired in a later
  release, not as part of namespace cleanup.

  Both paths are matched by name, so a rename on either side silently unprotects
  it with nothing else failing. android_backup_exclusion_test.dart is what makes
  that loud.

  RECHECK AFTER THE PRIVATE-DIRECTORY MIGRATION: once Core state actually lives
  in pocketclaw/ and the compatibility window is closed, the picoclaw/ line is
  retired deliberately — and only then.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="file" path="credentials/" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="pocketclaw/" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="picoclaw/" />
</full-backup-content>
